    (3, None, '\'I was just told the craziest news.\' "Mne rasskazali", "to me they told", is how Russian says "I was told", with "they" left out. "Bezumny", "crazy", is "bez", "without", plus "um", "mind". "Tolko chto" is "just now".'),
    (4, None, '\'Did the Chef want to kick you out of the kitchen again?\' Out of a place is "s" plus a changed ending: "s kukhni", "off the kitchen". "Vygnat", "to drive out", starts with vy-, the "out" prefix. "Snova" is "again": Kostya assumes it is the chef.'),
    (5, None, '\'No, the Chef has absolutely nothing to do with it.\' "Ni pri chyom" is the set phrase for "has nothing to do with it". "Sovershenno" is "absolutely". It\'s a handy phrase to learn whole.'),
    (6, None, '\'Then speak quickly, do not torment me for long.\' "Ne muchay" is "don\'t torment", a strong word that Kostya uses half joking. "Skoree", "quickly", comes from Max\'s "govorite skoree", "tell me quickly". "Govori" is "speak".'),
    (7, None, '\'Vika is pregnant with my child.\' "Beremenna ot menya" is "pregnant from me": Max uses Vika\'s own words, with "ot tebya" turned into "ot menya". It\'s just four words.'),
    (8, None, '\'What? Our beautiful Vika is expecting your child?\' "Zhdyot rebyonka", literally "waits for a child", is "is expecting a baby". "Zhdat" needs no word for "for": "rebyonok" just changes to "rebyonka". Kostya says "nasha Vika", "our Vika".'),
    (9, None, '\'Yes, she told me that right now.\' "Skazala" is "she said", with -la for a woman. "Pryamo seychas", literally "straight now", is "right now". "Mne eto" is "me that".'),
